{
  "_comment": "State machine of src/status_listener.js and timers of src/keep_alive_worker.js. SCENARIO groups (first_poll, armed_codes, sensor_codes, transitions, zone_indexing, failures): { name, zones, polls, expected_events, known_bug?, note? }. zones are the [enabled, name] tuples handed to statusListener() (already filtered by login(); the listener only uses their count and order). polls has one entry per 1000 ms tick: { statuszone, useraccess } makes the panel serve those tables (status code per zone; area state codes, first entry is area 1; tables have 0 or >= 2 entries because a one-entry table is a JavaScript new Array(n)); { page: { file } } serves that captured page (relative to tests/); { fail } makes the panel misbehave for this tick only: { status: n, body? } HTTP answer with that status (default body: a short error text), { connection: \"refused\" | \"reset\" } transport failure, { page: { file } } HTTP 200 with a page that has no status tables. expected_events lists, in emission order over the whole run: [\"armedChanged\", true | false | null], [\"sensorChanged\", \"<zone index as a decimal string>\", true | false], [\"error\"]. Before the first poll the listener assumes armed = null and every sensor = false; it never announces that state, only differences from it. CADENCE groups (status_poll_cadence, keep_alive_cadence): { name, advance_ms: [...], expected_requests: [...] } advance a fake clock step by step from the creation of the listener or worker and give the cumulative number of requests the panel has seen after each step (status polls: GET /statuslive.html; keep-alives: GET /keep_alive.html?msgid=1). keep_alive_failures: { name, ticks, expected_error_logged } one entry per 3000 ms tick, a { fail } as above or {}, and per tick whether the failure was reported (logged) by the worker. keep_alive_random: what Math.random() returns, and the only request ever sent. \"known_bug\" refers to the catalog in README.md;",
